    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d601">

                <pb facs="0678" n="660" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg660"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">821.243/11–1346</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Acting Chief of the Division of
                        Special Inter-American Affairs</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dreier</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Director of the Office of American Republic Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Briggs</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-11-13">November 13, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Colonel Ordway<anchor n="27" xml:id="d601-f1"/> came over to see you this morning
                    and, finding you busy, dropped in to tell me that he wished to report he had
                    orders from the Chief of Staff not to send the tear gas bombs, etc., to Colombia
                    unless he had a written request from the State Department. Your letter in which
                    you said the State Department endorsed the request of Ambassador Wiley did not
                    meet this requirement.</p>
                <p>Colonel Ordway said that General Crittenberger<anchor n="28" xml:id="d601-f2"/>
                    was under orders to hold the material ready. A telegram to CDC ordering the shipment to Bogotá is already
                    drafted and ready to go as soon as a written request is received from the State
                    Department.</p>
                <p>I told Colonel Ordway that we appreciated his coming over on this matter, and
                    that I would send you a memorandum conveying his message. <pb facs="0679"
                        n="661" xml:id="pg661"/> I suggested that he sit on this matter until he had
                    further word from the Department, either making the request or calling it
                    off.</p>
                <p>In view of the somewhat more favorable turn of events in Colombia, I believe we
                    should inform Ambassador Wiley and the War Department that we consider this
                    shipment inadvisable. To turn over property of the US Army to another government
                    for the purpose of controlling internal labor conflicts is certainly a dangerous
                    and undesirable move under any circumstances.</p>
                <closer><signed>J. C. D[<hi rendition="#small-caps">reier</hi>]</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
